WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.372 --> 00:00:05.132
Steve Jobs turned Apple into one of the biggest
companies in the world.

00:00:05.132 --> 00:00:10.212
So when he passed away in 2011, people wondered:
What will this mean for Apple?

00:00:10.212 --> 00:00:11.212
Good morning.

00:00:11.212 --> 00:00:12.212
Good morning.

00:00:12.212 --> 00:00:14.572
This is my first product launch as CEO.

00:00:14.572 --> 00:00:17.973
I’m sure you didn’t know that.

00:00:17.973 --> 00:00:23.003
When Tim Cook took the stage in June 2011
for a special event to introduce a new iPhone,

00:00:23.003 --> 00:00:28.683
a seat was reserved in the front for Jobs…in
case he was feeling well enough to attend.

00:00:28.683 --> 00:00:32.983
But diagnosed with pancreatic cancer, Jobs’
health had worsened.

00:00:32.983 --> 00:00:36.873
The following day, he died at the age of 56.

00:00:36.873 --> 00:00:42.502
Apple’s chief designer Jony Ive skipped
the event to be at Jobs’s side, as told

00:00:42.502 --> 00:00:47.703
in the book “After Steve” by Wall Street
Journal correspondent Tripp Mickle.

00:00:47.703 --> 00:00:52.803
Jobs considered Ive the second-most important
person at Apple, after himself.

00:00:52.803 --> 00:00:55.053
Hey Jony

00:00:55.053 --> 00:00:58.403
They had lunch together nearly every day.

00:00:58.403 --> 00:01:00.534
A typical conversation would go like this:

00:01:00.534 --> 00:01:06.313
“Steve used to say to me, and he used to
say this a lot, ‘Hey, Jony, here’s a dopey

00:01:06.313 --> 00:01:07.313
idea.’

00:01:07.313 --> 00:01:10.783
And sometimes they were.”

00:01:10.783 --> 00:01:14.862
Jobs gave Ive the freedom for his creative
juices to flow.

00:01:14.862 --> 00:01:19.813
Ive was inspired by the godfather of design
Dieter Rams’s philosophy of creating simple

00:01:19.813 --> 00:01:21.633
and functional products.

00:01:21.633 --> 00:01:28.473
Yet, after Jobs died, Apple appeared to shift
its focus from design to prioritizing profit.

00:01:28.473 --> 00:01:32.033
Cook made Apple wealthy beyond imagination.

00:01:32.033 --> 00:01:39.832
The company’s annual revenue quadrupled
from $108.2 billion in 2011 to $394.3 billion

00:01:39.832 --> 00:01:41.503
in 2022.

00:01:41.503 --> 00:01:46.303
Cook made significant inroads in China and
expanded Apple’s services business.

00:01:46.303 --> 00:01:50.783
But despite the expansion of services and
the introduction of new products, THIS is

00:01:50.783 --> 00:01:53.643
still Apple’s most valuable product.

00:01:53.643 --> 00:01:58.783
And we are calling it iPhone.

00:01:58.783 --> 00:02:03.203
The iPhone still accounts for more than half
of Apple’s revenue!

00:02:03.203 --> 00:02:08.242
Over the years, the iPhone’s camera improved,
and so have its chips, yet its design has

00:02:08.242 --> 00:02:10.563
remained pretty much the same.

00:02:10.563 --> 00:02:13.213
To the point that Samsung mocked Apple in
this commercial:

00:02:13.213 --> 00:02:17.573
Clip And that epic moon shot that’s getting
all the likes won’t be yours.

00:02:17.573 --> 00:02:22.453
Because this innovation is not coming soon
to an iPhone near you.

00:02:22.453 --> 00:02:24.893
It’s already here in the Galaxy.

00:02:24.893 --> 00:02:29.382
Athough Apple became richer under Cook, the
company has been criticized for being slow

00:02:29.382 --> 00:02:30.602
to innovate.

00:02:30.602 --> 00:02:35.022
Former Apple sales executive David Sobotta
told the tech news website Readwrite:

00:02:35.022 --> 00:02:37.963
"Technology-wise, I think Tim Cook is a lightweight.

00:02:37.963 --> 00:02:42.923
I never felt passion for technology from Tim
like I did from Steve and some of the great

00:02:42.923 --> 00:02:44.063
engineers.”

00:02:44.063 --> 00:02:49.893
Former Apple engineer Bob Burrough told CNBC
that the “dynamic has clearly and distinctly

00:02:49.893 --> 00:02:51.803
changed”.

00:02:51.803 --> 00:02:54.973
Jobs was known for constantly pushing the
design team.

00:02:54.973 --> 00:02:59.233
He once walked past a prototype of an upcoming
iPhone that he didn’t like, and said to

00:02:59.233 --> 00:03:06.313
an engineer: “What is this sh*&amp;?”, a remark
characteristic of his candid demeanor.

00:03:06.313 --> 00:03:10.783
Jobs also insisted that the iPhone needed
to be changed from plastic to glass, even

00:03:10.783 --> 00:03:14.373
though the design team worried that glass
would crack easily.

00:03:14.373 --> 00:03:19.034
Jobs insisted and told Jeff Williams, Apple’s
Chief Operating Officer, “I don’t know

00:03:19.034 --> 00:03:23.933
how we’re going to do it, but when it ships
in June, it’s going to be glass.”

00:03:23.933 --> 00:03:29.583
When Ive presented the initial iPad prototypes
to Jobs, the rectangular design seemed too

00:03:29.583 --> 00:03:30.583
harsh.

00:03:30.583 --> 00:03:35.263
They opted to round the edges, making it easier
to slide a finger underneath and lift from

00:03:35.263 --> 00:03:36.893
a table.

00:03:36.893 --> 00:03:41.863
Jobs spent much of his time discussing prototypes
by the long wooden tables outside Ive’s

00:03:41.863 --> 00:03:42.863
office.

00:03:42.863 --> 00:03:46.253
On the other hand, Cook hardly swung by the
design studio.

00:03:46.253 --> 00:03:48.473
And when he did, it wasn’t for long.

00:03:48.473 --> 00:03:53.173
Cook explained to ESPN: “I knew what I needed
to do was not to mimic him.”

00:03:53.173 --> 00:03:58.102
“I would fail miserably at that, and I think
this is largely the case for many people who

00:03:58.102 --> 00:04:00.983
take a baton from someone larger than life.

00:04:00.983 --> 00:04:02.793
You have to chart your own course.

00:04:02.793 --> 00:04:05.893
You have to be the best version of yourself.”

00:04:05.893 --> 00:04:09.483
He decided to focus on business and operations.

00:04:09.483 --> 00:04:13.283
Cook would wake up before 4 am to review sales
data.

00:04:13.283 --> 00:04:18.873
He’d want to know why a certain iPhone model
was outselling another model in a given city.

00:04:18.873 --> 00:04:23.813
Before becoming CEO, he was Apple’s chief
operating officer, responsible for managing

00:04:23.813 --> 00:04:25.572
the global supply chain.

00:04:25.572 --> 00:04:31.743
He’s credited with streamlining Apple’s
manufacturing process, which cut costs significantly.

00:04:31.743 --> 00:04:35.973
He minimized the amount of inventory Apple
held at any given time, something he learned

00:04:35.973 --> 00:04:41.003
at IBM…where he was responsible for securing
components to make PCs.

00:04:41.003 --> 00:04:44.732
He once declared: “Inventory is fundamentally
evil.”

00:04:44.732 --> 00:04:49.452
Likening it to dairy products; he believed
they should be viewed as perishable and not

00:04:49.452 --> 00:04:51.893
be allowed to sit around for too long.

00:04:51.893 --> 00:04:55.093
There wasn’t much doubt who Jobs would pick
to succeed him.

00:04:55.093 --> 00:04:58.553
Cook exuded calm and listened more than he
spoke.

00:04:58.553 --> 00:05:03.703
Ive reportedly did not want the top job and
was more comfortable focusing on design rather

00:05:03.703 --> 00:05:06.403
than the business side of running a company.

00:05:06.403 --> 00:05:12.053
The only other serious contender for CEO was
Scott Forstall the head of software design.

00:05:12.053 --> 00:05:16.003
However, his ego and history of conflicts
ruled him out.

00:05:16.003 --> 00:05:21.273
And then Cook pushed him out of the company
after the disastrous launch of Maps that Forstall

00:05:21.273 --> 00:05:22.273
oversaw.

00:05:22.273 --> 00:05:23.853
And it is beautiful.

00:05:23.853 --> 00:05:26.013
The maps were rife with errors.

00:05:26.013 --> 00:05:28.542
Cook even had to issue an apology.

00:05:28.542 --> 00:05:33.102
With Forstall gone, Cook handed oversight
of software design to Ive, which put more

00:05:33.102 --> 00:05:34.743
on Ive’s plate.

00:05:34.743 --> 00:05:38.743
One of Jony’s big ideas was adding the control
center that lets you swipe from the top or

00:05:38.743 --> 00:05:40.893
bottom of the screen.

00:05:40.893 --> 00:05:42.863
Engineers originally said it couldn’t be
done.

00:05:42.863 --> 00:05:45.843
But Ive insisted.

00:05:45.843 --> 00:05:50.383
After Jobs’ died, there were critics who
questioned Apple’s ability to innovate.

00:05:50.383 --> 00:05:55.852
They desperately needed a product to wow the
world and silence the doubters.

00:05:55.852 --> 00:06:02.283
In 2012, Ive arranged for Apple’s top executives
to meet at a hotel in San Francisco to discuss

00:06:02.283 --> 00:06:05.422
a wearable device with health sensors.

00:06:05.422 --> 00:06:08.743
Noticeably absent from the meeting…was Tim
Cook.

00:06:08.743 --> 00:06:13.633
He sent a message saying he had no plans to
try to fill the shoes of his predecessor.

00:06:13.633 --> 00:06:18.982
Cook delegated responsibility to Ive and to
the head of operations, Jeff Williams.

00:06:18.982 --> 00:06:23.732
The Apple Watch was the company’s most personal
product, and as a result, it came with different

00:06:23.732 --> 00:06:27.593
watch bands and various colors so people could
customize it.

00:06:27.593 --> 00:06:32.452
The digital crown to expand and contract the
app icons paid homage to the traditional watch

00:06:32.452 --> 00:06:35.083
design and blended together the past and the
future.

00:06:35.083 --> 00:06:36.083
It is the next chapter in Apple’s story.

00:06:36.083 --> 00:06:37.083
And here it is.

00:06:37.083 --> 00:06:48.053
Cook unveiled the Apple Watch in September
2014, three years after Jobs’ death.

00:06:48.053 --> 00:06:54.123
Although the excitement was palpable at the
launch event, the media’s reaction was lukewarm.

00:06:54.123 --> 00:06:59.403
A Bloomberg headline put it this way: “You’ll
Want One, but You Don’t Need One.”

00:06:59.403 --> 00:07:04.813
Ive anticipated Apple needed to make 40 million
watches in the first year to meet demand,

00:07:04.813 --> 00:07:08.943
but Apple only sold around 12 million watches
in 2015.

00:07:08.943 --> 00:07:14.343
The Apple Watch did gain traction over time,
and Cook said it leapfrogged Rolex to become

00:07:14.343 --> 00:07:16.793
the most popular watch on earth.

00:07:16.793 --> 00:07:21.993
The Apple Watch sapped a lot of energy out
of Ive, who had to oversee industrial design,

00:07:21.993 --> 00:07:27.423
software design, and marketing - all without
the collaboration of his creative soulmate.

00:07:27.423 --> 00:07:28.423
(show Jobs)

00:07:28.423 --> 00:07:32.732
Ive felt as if he had to fight hard for his
vision, like convincing the marketing team

00:07:32.732 --> 00:07:38.132
that the watch needed to be marketed as a
fashion accessory rather than as a tech device.

00:07:38.132 --> 00:07:43.523
The watch would eventually shift from fashion
to fitness in an effort to boost sales.

00:07:43.523 --> 00:07:48.632
Ive told the New Yorker that he’d become
“deeply, deeply tired” and that the year

00:07:48.632 --> 00:07:52.743
leading up to the Watch’s debut had been
“the most difficult” he’d ever spent

00:07:52.743 --> 00:07:53.743
at Apple.

00:07:53.743 --> 00:07:58.883
Just like Cook’s ability to squeeze more
out of suppliers, the CEO appeared to be squeezing

00:07:58.883 --> 00:08:04.533
more out of Ive…who took a three-week vacation
in Hawaii to unwind - the longest break he’d

00:08:04.533 --> 00:08:05.723
had in years.

00:08:05.723 --> 00:08:06.723
(Kauai)

00:08:06.723 --> 00:08:11.023
Tripp Mickle’s book describes how Ive wanted
to leave Apple, which alarmed Cook, who didn’t

00:08:11.023 --> 00:08:14.632
want to lose the world’s most famous industrial
designer.

00:08:14.632 --> 00:08:19.572
The solution was for Ive to step back from
day-to-day management and shift to a part-time

00:08:19.572 --> 00:08:24.873
role, focusing on future projects such as
designing Apple’s spaceship-like headquarters.

00:08:24.873 --> 00:08:27.893
Two designers who reported to him would be
given more daily responsibilities.

00:08:27.893 --> 00:08:29.442
(Richard Howarth and Alan Dye)

00:08:29.442 --> 00:08:34.843
Then there was the question of what would
be Apple’s next big thing.

00:08:34.843 --> 00:08:39.553
Launching a successful electric car would
blow away any doubt about Apple’s ability

00:08:39.553 --> 00:08:40.553
to innovate.

00:08:40.553 --> 00:08:45.203
At the time, Tesla was doubling its staff
and developing more sophisticated batteries

00:08:45.203 --> 00:08:46.993
for its electric vehicles.

00:08:46.993 --> 00:08:51.043
Tesla was also recruiting dozens of Apple
engineers.

00:08:51.043 --> 00:08:55.443
Jobs had reportedly shown interest in building
an Apple car as early as 2008.

00:08:55.443 --> 00:09:01.093
Apple hired hundreds of engineers to start
working on the secretive “Project Titan”.

00:09:01.093 --> 00:09:06.073
Ive envisioned a fully self-driving car where
passengers would hop in and tell Siri where

00:09:06.073 --> 00:09:08.143
to take them, and off they went.

00:09:08.143 --> 00:09:13.403
But, as Apple found out, building an autonomous
car is extremely difficult.

00:09:13.403 --> 00:09:17.793
And there was no passionate leader like Jobs
to spearhead things.

00:09:17.793 --> 00:09:23.363
Apple’s research and development spending
in 2015 hit $8 billion, with the secretive

00:09:23.363 --> 00:09:26.544
car project undoubtedly eating up a huge chunk
of that budget.

00:09:26.544 --> 00:09:32.273
As it stands now, reliable Apple analyst Ming-Chi
Kuo believes it's unlikely an Apple car will

00:09:32.273 --> 00:09:36.062
hit the market before 2025.

00:09:36.062 --> 00:09:39.622
Apple then turned its attention to another
product.

00:09:39.622 --> 00:09:43.923
Wireless earbuds would further free people
from their iPhones, as the Apple Watch had

00:09:43.923 --> 00:09:44.923
done.

00:09:44.923 --> 00:09:49.413
Ive’s design team had brainstormed the idea
of AirPods back in 2013.

00:09:49.413 --> 00:09:55.403
When they first came out, people thought they
looked a little ridiculous.

00:09:55.403 --> 00:09:59.703
Comedian Conan O’Brien poked fun at their
potential for falling out of peoples’ ears.

00:09:59.703 --> 00:10:00.703
Really?

00:10:00.703 --> 00:10:01.703
Ugh, fine

00:10:01.703 --> 00:10:05.793
Despite the issues, the AirPods quickly gained
popularity for their sound quality and seamless

00:10:05.793 --> 00:10:08.203
integration with the Apple ecosystem.

00:10:08.203 --> 00:10:12.513
Yet, it was clear Ive’s influence was diminishing.

00:10:12.513 --> 00:10:16.993
He had little to no say when Cook selected
a new board member to replace a longtime confidant

00:10:16.993 --> 00:10:19.333
of his, Mickey Drexler.

00:10:19.333 --> 00:10:24.233
Cook named James Bell, Boeing’s former chief
financial officer, to Apple’s Board, which

00:10:24.233 --> 00:10:26.473
addressed a lack of diversity.

00:10:26.473 --> 00:10:31.823
Ive supported diversifying the Board but preferred
someone with marketing expertise over finance.

00:10:31.823 --> 00:10:37.133
Mickle’s book says a colleague defending
the pick of Bell told Ive, “He’s an underrepresented

00:10:37.133 --> 00:10:38.133
minority.

00:10:38.133 --> 00:10:39.622
He has a great reputation.”

00:10:39.622 --> 00:10:42.383
Ive reportedly responded: “Who cares?

00:10:42.383 --> 00:10:44.633
We have to be concerned about the company.

00:10:44.633 --> 00:10:47.093
He’s another one of those accountants.”

00:10:47.093 --> 00:10:51.523
The appointment could be seen as demonstrating
Cook’s political adeptness.

00:10:51.523 --> 00:10:56.323
He was Apple’s chief diplomat; he charmed
the pants off Chinese ministers to secure

00:10:56.323 --> 00:11:01.453
government approval to use the iPhone on the
country’s largest wireless carrier.

00:11:01.453 --> 00:11:06.383
He navigated in an environment in which President
Trump vowed to bring jobs back to America

00:11:06.383 --> 00:11:10.773
which was worrying for a company that benefitted
from China’s cheap labor.

00:11:10.773 --> 00:11:15.093
Cook had masterminded the outsourcing of Apple's
production to China through the Taiwanese

00:11:15.093 --> 00:11:17.173
company Foxconn.

00:11:17.173 --> 00:11:22.543
There was significant controversy after a
spate of suicides in the 2000s and 2010s tied

00:11:22.543 --> 00:11:26.193
to brutal working conditions in Foxconn factories.

00:11:26.193 --> 00:11:29.742
Nets were installed to catch people as they
fell.

00:11:29.742 --> 00:11:34.303
Apple has taken steps to address the working
conditions, including conducting regular audits

00:11:34.303 --> 00:11:35.763
and inspections.

00:11:35.763 --> 00:11:42.473
85% of Apple’s Pro lineup of iPhones is
reportedly made in ‘iPhone city’, a sprawling

00:11:42.473 --> 00:11:47.663
campus in Zhengzhou, China, that employs as
many as 300,000 people.

00:11:47.663 --> 00:11:54.003
Depending heavily on China hurt Apple as COVID
restrictions delayed iPhone shipments.

00:11:54.003 --> 00:11:58.733
Apple realized it had to diversify and is
now trying to open up iPhone cities in India

00:11:58.733 --> 00:12:00.783
and Vietnam.

00:12:00.783 --> 00:12:05.422
Cook also had to manage many issues unrelated
to product development.

00:12:05.422 --> 00:12:10.243
Like the time the FBI demanded Apple unlock
an iPhone used by one of the shooters in the

00:12:10.243 --> 00:12:13.443
San Bernardino attack in 2015.

00:12:13.443 --> 00:12:16.503
Apple resisted, citing user privacy concerns.

00:12:16.503 --> 00:12:20.513
The FBI managed to access the data through
the help of a third party.

00:12:20.513 --> 00:12:27.523
And in 2020, Fortnite creator Epic Games sued
Apple, accusing it of anti-competitive practices.

00:12:27.523 --> 00:12:32.393
Apple had removed Fortnite from the App Store
after Epic Games allowed in-app purchases

00:12:32.393 --> 00:12:36.573
in Fortnite, bypassing Apple's 30% commission.

00:12:36.573 --> 00:12:41.473
So while Tim Cook was very busy, he spent
little time coming up with Apple’s next

00:12:41.473 --> 00:12:42.682
big product.

00:12:42.682 --> 00:12:47.552
Instead, Apple made changes to its existing
lineup, like removing the home button for

00:12:47.552 --> 00:12:49.302
the iPhone X.

00:12:49.302 --> 00:12:54.843
But the iPhone X was also delayed due to issues
with the facial recognition system.

00:12:54.843 --> 00:13:01.203
Let’s try that again.

00:13:01.203 --> 00:13:05.713
The delays coincided with Ive not being in
the office every day.

00:13:05.713 --> 00:13:10.363
According to Mickle’s book, Ive moved some
meetings off campus, to a private club in

00:13:10.363 --> 00:13:14.243
San Francisco, which apparently irked some
Apple executives.

00:13:14.243 --> 00:13:18.743
They were also reportedly not happy that Ive
was believed to be getting paid much more

00:13:18.743 --> 00:13:19.743
than them.

00:13:19.743 --> 00:13:25.443
Each member of the executive team earned about
$25 million annually, which was publicly disclosed

00:13:25.443 --> 00:13:28.003
to comply with the SEC's requirements.

00:13:28.003 --> 00:13:33.603
However, Ive avoided being classified as an
officer, and as a result, his compensation

00:13:33.603 --> 00:13:35.543
was kept secret.

00:13:35.543 --> 00:13:39.583
Some senior Apple employees felt the best
days were behind them.

00:13:39.583 --> 00:13:44.533
When top software designer Imran Chaudhri
prepared to leave the company in 2016, he

00:13:44.533 --> 00:13:49.982
sent a farewell email and quoted the poet
Rumi: “When you do things from your soul,

00:13:49.982 --> 00:13:53.163
you feel a river moving in you, a joy.”

00:13:53.163 --> 00:13:59.622
He then played off that line by writing: “Sadly,
rivers dry out, and when they do, you look

00:13:59.622 --> 00:14:01.432
for a new one.”

00:14:01.432 --> 00:14:07.103
The comments infuriated management, including
Ive, and Chauhdri was fired.

00:14:07.103 --> 00:14:11.893
Another employee who left, Chris Stringer,
was a top designer unhappy with Apple’s

00:14:11.893 --> 00:14:13.353
smart speaker.

00:14:13.353 --> 00:14:18.473
He believed Apple treated the HomePod like
an afterthought rather than a signature product.

00:14:18.473 --> 00:14:24.513
The HomePod has lagged far behind the popularity
of Amazon’s Echo and Google’s Nest.

00:14:24.513 --> 00:14:29.742
Ive realized how much his team struggled without
him and decided to return to a more hands-on

00:14:29.742 --> 00:14:31.732
role in late 2017.

00:14:31.732 --> 00:14:34.713
Yet…it was never the same again.

00:14:34.713 --> 00:14:38.663
The successors to the iPhone X ten looked
almost identical.

00:14:38.663 --> 00:14:43.783
Cook had hoped the lower-quality XR would
do well in China, but it bombed partly due

00:14:43.783 --> 00:14:46.253
to competition from Huawei.

00:14:46.253 --> 00:14:47.693
In January 2019, Apple released a letter from
Cook to investors, reducing the company’s

00:14:47.693 --> 00:14:48.693
quarterly sales estimate for the first time
in 16 years.

00:14:48.693 --> 00:14:53.742
Apple lowered revenue guidance to $84 billion
instead of the $89 billion to $93 billion

00:14:53.742 --> 00:14:55.682
it had previously projected.

00:14:55.682 --> 00:15:00.773
Cook blamed the economy and trade sanctions,
but the reality was, people were also getting

00:15:00.773 --> 00:15:04.783
tired of only incremental changes to the iPhone.

00:15:04.783 --> 00:15:08.713
Apple noticed customers who usually upgraded
their phones every two years were not doing

00:15:08.713 --> 00:15:11.193
so nearly as often.

00:15:11.193 --> 00:15:16.313
Despite the challenges, Apple had a milestone
to look forward to that year, in 2019, as

00:15:16.313 --> 00:15:20.543
it prepared to celebrate the formal opening
of its new headquarters in May.

00:15:20.543 --> 00:15:25.052
However, a day before, Ive’s dad had a stroke.

00:15:25.052 --> 00:15:29.993
He rushed to the UK to be with his father,
who thankfully recovered.

00:15:29.993 --> 00:15:34.992
Jony owed his career to his father, who had
taught design at UK schools and nurtured his

00:15:34.992 --> 00:15:37.093
interest in design.

00:15:37.093 --> 00:15:42.473
For Jony, it appeared time to consider returning
to the UK for good with his wife and twin

00:15:42.473 --> 00:15:43.703
boys.

00:15:43.703 --> 00:15:49.393
A month later, in June 2019, Ive gathered
a group of his designers and told them he

00:15:49.393 --> 00:15:51.862
was leaving Apple.

00:15:51.862 --> 00:15:55.602
He explained he’d grown tired of the company’s
bureaucracy, as reported by Tripp Mickle.

00:15:55.602 --> 00:15:59.713
Mickle writes in his book that Ive told the
team: “I don’t want to go to any more

00:15:59.713 --> 00:16:01.333
f*&amp;^ing meetings.”

00:16:01.333 --> 00:16:05.413
The design team would now report to the chief
operating officer, Jeff Williams.

00:16:05.413 --> 00:16:10.982
Ive’s foot wasn’t fully out the door,
however; he set up his own design firm alongside

00:16:10.982 --> 00:16:16.542
Marc Newson, an influential designer at Apple
who worked under his guidance.

00:16:16.542 --> 00:16:18.753
LoveFrom would consult for Apple.

00:16:18.753 --> 00:16:23.263
The contract was reportedly worth $100 million.

00:16:23.263 --> 00:16:27.973
Although Ive hoped the relationship between
his firm and Apple would last for “many,

00:16:27.973 --> 00:16:33.353
many years to come”, as he told the Financial
Times, that would not be the case.

00:16:33.353 --> 00:16:39.133
In 2022, Apple and Ive parted ways for good.

00:16:39.133 --> 00:16:44.463
Steve Jobs was well aware that great companies
faltered after their founders departed.

00:16:44.463 --> 00:16:49.143
It happened to Disney after the absence of
Walt’s leadership, and to Sony when co-founder

00:16:49.143 --> 00:16:52.513
Akio Morita stepped down as chairman.

00:16:52.513 --> 00:16:57.922
Jobs told his biographer Walter Isaacson:
“The company does a great job, innovates

00:16:57.922 --> 00:17:02.582
and becomes a monopoly or close to it in some
field, and then the quality of the product

00:17:02.582 --> 00:17:04.322
becomes less important.

00:17:04.322 --> 00:17:08.393
The company starts valuing the great salesmen,
because they’re the ones who can move the

00:17:08.393 --> 00:17:12.573
needle on revenues, not the product engineers
and designers.

00:17:12.573 --> 00:17:15.803
So the salespeople end up running the company.”

00:17:15.803 --> 00:17:22.163
Has his worst fear come true?

